Per the study-context operationalization that demographic growth is the
pollen_grate_100 series (percentage change of the 100-yr-smoothed openness
score pollen_inter_100), the highest-growth century of the 2000-1100 BCE
window is the 15th century BCE (1500-1401 BCE), where openness grew at
+1.89e-03 per year on the primary metric, more than 1.7x the second-best
century (1400-1301 BCE, +1.11e-03 per year), and roughly 2.2x the third
(1700-1601 BCE, +8.59e-04 per year). Robustness under three independent
aggregation rules confirms this single-century peak: (A) the bin's slope of
log(1+pollen_inter_100) is +7.81e-03 per year with R^2 = 0.86, the highest
of all nine bins (second-best 1700-1601 BCE at +2.94e-03); (B) the cumulative
2-century window starting 1500-1401 BCE (1500-1301 BCE) reaches +3.00e-03,
the max across all 8 candidate windows, and the cumulative 3-century window
starting 1500-1401 BCE (1500-1201 BCE) reaches +2.84e-03, also the max.
Openness growth thus appears as a sustained phase from roughly 1500 to
1301 BCE rather than a one-century spike; it collapses in the following
century (1300-1201 BCE: mean_grate = -1.67e-04). Cultural/economic
concordance is strong: 6 of the 12 capital Z categories rose within the
1500-1401 BCE bin (CopperGold +2.87 sigma, Dagger +1.92 sigma,
MonumentCount +1.53 sigma, AxesCelts +1.31 sigma, HouseSize +0.66 sigma,
Amber +0.14 sigma), giving a mean per-category within-bin Z delta of
+0.67 sigma - the single highest bin-average concordance across the 2000 to
1100 BCE window. Two independent demographic-density proxies confirm:
kde_all_mean (3.26e-04) and g_all_mean (+2.83e-03 per year) are the highest
in the window and peak at the same century. The result is robust to (i) the
1100 BCE boundary treatment (including CE=-1100 in the last century shifts
its mean from -1.036e-03 to -1.051e-03 and does not change the rank-1
century); and (ii) alternative openness-kernel smoothing (pol_file
SMOOTH_MEAN_200y gives the 1500-1401 BCE bin slope +3.00e-03/yr, again
rank-1). The 1500-1401 BCE peak also sits between the LN/OBA transition
(LN ends 1700 BCE; OBA 1600-1200 BCE per capital.csv groupings) - i.e., it
falls in the very earliest OBA horizon, consistent with capital-Z
co-acceleration.
